  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) The paper proposes developing and evaluating PLMeAE, a closed-loop protein engineering platform that uses protein language model guidance to prioritize high-fitness mutants under different mutation-site scenarios and couples this prediction with automated biofoundry construction and screening to accelerate directed evolution, including application to Methanocaldococcus jannaschii p-cyanophenylalanine tRNA synthetase.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When directed evolution and related protein engineering methods face practical bottlenecks, and protein language models and biofoundries offer complementary prediction and automation capabilities, a closed-loop platform that integrates prediction with automated construction and screening is a plausible research strategy to test."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Targeted mutagenesis has a practical limitation because its success depends strongly on choosing appropriate mutation sites."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Experimental acquisition of functional protein data is a time- and labor-intensive bottleneck for data-driven protein engineering approaches."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Current protein engineering has an additional opportunity and limitation in that it has not fully leveraged recent biofoundry developments."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Biofoundry automation for large data volumes and protein language model predictive strength motivate treating these capabilities as complementary for protein engineering."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) The incompletely used biofoundry capability and the complementary roles of biofoundry automation and protein language model prediction motivate investigating an integrated PLM-biofoundry strategy for protein engineering."];
